{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_f2f939c6e7e44c879e5e6a2c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Spec left at its current status (`generating`) — the daemon's generator owns the `prompted` transition in Mode 1.\n\n## Generated Prompts\n\n**Prompt-creator output** (`dark-factory:prompt-creator`, agent `aeec503807722fc25`):\n\n**Files created** (both in the inbox `prompts/`, not `prompts/in-progress/`):\n- `/workspace/prompts/1-spec-001-fix-nil-result.md` — implementation prompt (fix + regression tests)\n- `/workspace/prompts/2-spec-001-changelog-precommit.md` — finalization prompt (CHANGELOG + `make precommit`)\n\n**Decomposition decisions (why 2, not the spec's 4):**\n1. **Implementation + tests in one prompt** — the two entry-point changes are near-identical, coupled by the same defect, and must ship together (the spec's atomic-batch constraint says \"the fix lives in this repo's two entry points only\"). The regression tests belong with their implementation — splitting tests into their own prompt would have been a dependency-heavy no-op. The whole thing is ~20 lines of production change + two small Ginkgo specs.\n2. **CHANGELOG + `make precommit` in a separate final prompt** — the CLAUDE.md convention is to write the `## Unreleased` entry *before* the slow final gate, and AC 4/5 are distinct deliverables. Keeping the fast `make test` loop in prompt 1 separate from the slow precommit gate in prompt 2 is the natural split.\n\nPer the Sizing Guide, 2 prompts is right for a single feature (2-3 range).\n\n**Key design decisions (surfaced for the reviewer):**\n- **Test observability was the one real ambiguity.** `main.go`'s `application.Run` builds the deliverer internally (`createDeliverer`): empty `TASK_ID` → Noop (not inspectable), non-empty → real Kafka SyncProducer (needs a broker). So an end-to-end test through `Run` cannot assert on the delivered `AgentResultInfo` without a real Kafka broker or a new injection seam. Resolved by **extracting the nil-result delivery into an unexported `application.deliverNilResult(ctx, deliverer) error` method in both entry points** (mirroring the agent lib's `unsupportedPhase`), and unit-testing that method directly with the counterfeiter fake `github.com/bborbe/agent/mocks.AgentResultDeliverer` — the same external-mock pattern this repo already uses with `kafkamocks`. This asserts exactly what the ACs demand (`Status == Failed`, phase name in `Message`, no error) without Kafka. No new injection seam, no re-architecting.\n- **Test package changed `main_test` → `main`** in both `main_test.go` files (required to reach the unexported method). Deviates from the go-testing-guide's external-package preference; the standard tradeoff for testing unexported entry-point internals, rationale stated inline.\n- **Message text kept as-is**: `\"agent run returned nil result (all steps skipped for phase %s)\"` becomes the delivered `Message` — minimal diff, still names the phase.\n- **No `PrintResult` on the nil path** — mirrors `unsupportedPhase`; failure is diagnosable in the task body per desired behavior #3.\n- **Deliver failure** returns `errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"deliver nil-result failure\")` — matches the spec Failure Modes table's `deliver ... failure` log signature.\n- **No scenario prompt** — the behavior is fully unit-testable with the fake deliverer, so no `scenarios/NNN-*.md` is warranted; operator-executable deploy checks stay on the spec's Verification ladder.\n\n**Symbol verification (all read from real source before writing):** `agentlib.ResultDeliverer`/`DeliverResult`, `AgentResultInfo{Status, Output, Message, NextPhase, ContinueToNext}`, `AgentStatusFailed = \"failed\"`, `agentlib.Result`, `errors.Wrapf`/`errors.New` (bborbe/errors v1.5.21), `mocks.AgentResultDeliverer` fake API, plus a sibling entry-point check — a third `application.Run` in `cmd/create-tasks/main.go` never calls `agent.Run` and has no nil-result guard, explicitly marked out-of-sc